        },
        {
          "estimated_narration_seconds": 47,
          "kind": "concept",
          "lines": [
            {
              "fact_ids": [
                "sec07-F09"
              ],
              "text": "Force equals weight times speed."
            },
            {
              "fact_ids": [
                "sec07-F09"
              ],
              "text": "Bracing cannot resist crash force."
            },
            {
              "fact_ids": [
                "sec07-F09"
              ],
              "text": "Belts matter even when slow."
            }
          ],
          "media_absence_reason": "text_first_pilot",
          "media_role": "none",
          "narration": {
            "fact_ids": [
              "sec07-F09"
            ],
            "text": "Physics explains why belts matter at any speed. Crash forces equal body weight multiplied by vehicle speed, so a one hundred fifty-pound occupant in a thirty miles per hour crash experiences four thousand five hundred pounds of force that cannot be resisted by bracing. Arms cannot hold you, and slow streets do not make you safe. Only a properly worn belt can manage that load. Remember this number when a short slow trip tempts you to skip."
          },
          "slide_id": "sec07-u03-s04",
          "title": {
            "fact_ids": [
              "sec07-F09"
            ],
            "text": "Physics Demands The Belt"
          }
        },
        {
          "estimated_narration_seconds": 46,
          "kind": "bridge",
          "lines": [
            {
              "fact_ids": [
                "sec07-F10"
              ],
              "text": "One citation per unrestrained person."
